  "contracts": {
    "lem-qft-example-four-dimensional-radial-measure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $m\\ge1$, let U,V be open subsets of $\\mathbb R^m$, and let $T:U\\to V$ be a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Borel $h:V\\to[0,\\infty]$, $\\int_V h(y)\\,dy=\\int_U h(T(x))|\\det DT(x)|\\,dx$, with equality in $[0,\\infty]$ and $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. This statement concerns Borel h; no completed-measurable substitution is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Then:\n\n1. **Degenerate boxes.** If $a_i \\le b_i$ are reals with $a_{i_0} = b_{i_0}$ for\n   some $i_0 < n$, then every set $R$ between the open box\n   $\\{x : a_i < x_i < b_i\\ (i<n)\\}$ and the closed rectangle $[a,b]$ is Lebesgue\n   measurable with $\\lambda_n(R) = 0$.\n2. **Coordinate hyperplanes.** For $i_0 < n$ and a real $c$, the set\n   $$H_{i_0,c} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : x_{i_0} = c \\,\\}$$\n   is Lebesgue measurable with $\\lambda_n(H_{i_0,c}) = 0$.\n\nAt $n = 1$ the hyperplane $H_{0,c}$ is the singleton $\\{c\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n \\ge 1$,\nlet $S^{n-1} \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be the unit sphere, and let $\\sigma$ be the\nset function of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]. Then $\\sigma$\nis a finite Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$, and for every Borel measurable\n$f : \\mathbb R^n \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^n} f(x)\\,d\\lambda_n(x) = \\int_0^\\infty \\int_{S^{n-1}} f(r\\omega) r^{n-1}\\,d\\sigma(\\omega)\\,dr.$$\nMoreover, $\\sigma$ is the unique Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$ with this\nproperty.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$\n\n**Both hypotheses are needed and neither is removable.** A function may be\ndifferentiable everywhere with $G'$ not integrable — then the left-hand side does\nnot exist (an everywhere differentiable function with unbounded derivative) — and an\nintegrable $f$ need not be the derivative of anything\n(the sign function); both witnesses are on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**No continuity of $f$ is assumed**, which is what makes this the working form:\nthe theorem evaluates $\\int_a^b f$ for every integrable derivative, not only for\ncontinuous integrands.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x,\\qquad\\cos(-x)=\\cos x,\\qquad\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1.$$\nConsequently $|\\sin x|\\le1$ and $|\\cos x|\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Sine is differentiable, hence continuous, and strictly increasing on\n$[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]$; its endpoint values are $-1$ and $1$\n([[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]],\n[[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]],\n[[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]],\n[[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]],\n[[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]]). The intermediate value\ntheorem therefore makes its restricted image exactly $[-1,1]$\n([[thm-intermediate-value]]). Likewise, cosine is continuous and strictly\ndecreasing on $[0,\\pi]$, with endpoint values $1$ and $-1$, so its restricted\nimage is $[-1,1]$ ([[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]],\n[[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]],\n[[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[thm-intermediate-value]]).\nTheir principal inverses are denoted\n\n$$\\arcsin:[-1,1]\\to[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2],\\qquad\\arccos:[-1,1]\\to[0,\\pi],$$\n\nand are characterised by\n\n$$\\sin(\\arcsin y)=y,\\qquad\\cos(\\arccos y)=y\\qquad(-1\\le y\\le1).$$\n\nThe chosen target intervals are part of the notation: without them, inverse\nsine and inverse cosine would be multivalued relations rather than functions.\nTheir continuity follows from [[thm-continuous-inverse]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $-1<y<1$,\n\n$$(\\arcsin y)'=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}},\\qquad(\\arccos y)'=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Sine is strictly increasing on each interval $[-\\pi/2+2m\\pi,\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$ and strictly decreasing on each interval $[\\pi/2+2m\\pi,3\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$. Cosine is strictly decreasing on $[2m\\pi,(2m+1)\\pi]$ and strictly increasing on $[(2m+1)\\pi,(2m+2)\\pi]$. Both functions have range $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $a<b$ and let $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ be\nbounded and Riemann integrable. Then $f$ is Lebesgue measurable on $[a,b]$ and\nis integrable there, and its Lebesgue integral equals its Riemann integral:\n$$\\int_{[a,b]} f\\,d\\lambda_1=\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx.$$\n\nThis is the point at which the completeness of Lebesgue measure is used\nessentially: the proof obtains a Borel function equal to $f$ almost everywhere,\nand measurability of $f$ itself is then a completeness statement.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "On the open set\n$U=(0,R)\\times(0,\\pi/2)\\times(0,2\\pi)\\times(0,2\\pi)$ define\n$$ T(r,\\theta,\\phi,\\psi)= (r\\cos\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\ r\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\ r\\sin\\theta\\cos\\psi,\\ r\\sin\\theta\\sin\\psi). $$\nLet $L=\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(a,0):a\\ge0\\}$, the plane slit along\nthe nonnegative horizontal ray. For $(a,b)\\in L$, put\n$\\rho=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}>0$ and define $\\operatorname{Arg}_L(a,b)$ to be\n$\\arccos(a/\\rho)$ if $b>0$, $2\\pi-\\arccos(a/\\rho)$ if $b<0$, and\n$\\pi$ if $b=0$ (then $a<0$). The inverse and branch identities [F8]–[F10]\ngive $\\cos\\operatorname{Arg}_L=a/\\rho$ and\n$\\sin\\operatorname{Arg}_L=b/\\rho$ and make this the unique angle in\n$(0,2\\pi)$. Each non-axis branch is $C^1$ by [F9]. Near the negative\nhorizontal axis, the same angle equals $\\pi-\\arcsin(b/\\rho)$, so it is\n$C^1$ there too. Positive square roots are $C^1$: continuity follows from\n$|\\sqrt u-\\sqrt v|\\le\\sqrt{|u-v|}$, and the difference quotient is\n$1/(\\sqrt{u+h}+\\sqrt u)$, with continuous limit $1/(2\\sqrt u)$ for $u>0$.\nThus all radii in these formulas are $C^1$ by [F7].\nDefine the open set\n$V=\\{x:|x|<R,\\ (x_1,x_2)\\in L,\\ (x_3,x_4)\\in L\\}$.\nWrite $A=\\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2}>0$, $B=\\sqrt{x_3^2+x_4^2}>0$.\nThe inverse of $T$ on $V$ is explicitly\n$r=\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}$, $\\theta=\\arccos(A/r)\\in(0,\\pi/2)$,\n$\\phi=\\operatorname{Arg}_L(x_1,x_2)$,\n$\\psi=\\operatorname{Arg}_L(x_3,x_4)$.\nIndeed $\\cos\\theta=A/r$, $\\sin\\theta=B/r$ by [F8]–[F10], and substitution\ninto $T$ gives $x$; the same branch identities recover every parameter of\n$U$. Both directions are $C^1$, so $T:U\\to V$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism.\nUsing [F6]–[F8], The four derivative\ncolumns are pairwise orthogonal, with squared lengths\n$1,r^2,r^2\\cos^2\\theta,r^2\\sin^2\\theta$; hence\n$|\\det DT|=r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$. The complement of $V$ in the closed ball\nis contained in the union of the boundary sphere and the four coordinate\nhyperplanes: the omitted angular branch cuts lie in $x_2=0$ or $x_4=0$, and\nthe omitted endpoint planes lie in $x_1=x_2=0$ or $x_3=x_4=0$. The coordinate\nhyperplanes are null by [F2]. The boundary sphere is null by [F4], applied to\nits indicator, since its polar radial integral is supported at the singleton\n$\\{R\\}$, which has one-dimensional Lebesgue measure zero. The origin is\nalready in the coordinate hyperplanes.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1] to $T$ and the nonnegative Borel function\n$x\\mapsto\\varphi(|x|)$ on $V$. The null-complement calculation in step 1.1\nshows that its integral over $V$ equals its integral over the closed ball,\neven when $\\varphi$ takes the value $+\\infty$ on a radius whose preimage in\nthe omitted set is null. Therefore the ball integral equals\n$$ \\int_U \\varphi(r)r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\,d\\phi\\,d\\psi. $$",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The integrand in step 2.1 is nonnegative and product-measurable, so [F3]\nseparates the four integrations. The angular factors are\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta=1/2$ and\n$\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,d\\phi=\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,d\\psi=2\\pi$; the first equality follows\nfrom [F5] with antiderivative $\\sin^2\\theta/2$, whose derivative is $\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ by [F6]–[F7]. The angular integrands are continuous and bounded on their compact intervals, so [F11] identifies these calculus evaluations with the Lebesgue integrals in [F3]. Their product is $2\\pi^2$,\nwhich proves the formula in the Statement. Endpoints do not change any\nLebesgue integral, and all equalities are valid as extended nonnegative\nintegrals.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F11",
            "step 2.1",
