    "lem-qft-example-cutoff-integrals-and-series": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nUse the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. In the common natural-unit field convention, $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, a scalar field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and a Dirac field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation set $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$, with $A_a$ in tesla-metres and gauge function $\\chi$ in webers. With charge $q$ in coulombs define\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-four-dimensional-radial-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. For every radius $R>0$ and every\nnonnegative Borel function $\\varphi:[0,R]\\to[0,\\infty]$, with extended\nLebesgue integrals and the convention $0\\cdot\\infty=0$,\n$$ \\int_{\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^4:\\ |x|\\le R\\}}\\varphi(|x|)\\,d\\lambda_4(x) =2\\pi^2\\int_0^R\\varphi(r)r^3\\,dr. $$\nIn particular, the radial Jacobian and angular factor on a four-dimensional\nball are $r^3$ and $2\\pi^2$, respectively.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$\n\n**Both hypotheses are needed and neither is removable.** A function may be\ndifferentiable everywhere with $G'$ not integrable — then the left-hand side does\nnot exist (an everywhere differentiable function with unbounded derivative) — and an\nintegrable $f$ need not be the derivative of anything\n(the sign function); both witnesses are on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**No continuity of $f$ is assumed**, which is what makes this the working form:\nthe theorem evaluates $\\int_a^b f$ for every integrable derivative, not only for\ncontinuous integrands.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $z\\in\\mathbb C$, the series $\\sum z^n/n!$ converges absolutely.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-integration-by-parts",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $u, v : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at\nevery point of $[a,b]$ as functions on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]). Suppose\n$u'$ and $v'$ are integrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then $uv'$\nand $u'v$ are integrable and\n\n$$\\int_a^b u\\,v' \\;=\\; u(b)v(b) - u(a)v(a) \\;-\\; \\int_a^b u'\\,v .$$\n\n**The integrability of $u'$ and $v'$ is a hypothesis, not a formality.** Without\nit the two integrals in the display need not exist at all, and the identity is\nthen not false but ill-formed; that is\nthe false statement that deletes it on the companion\npage. The hypothesis is automatic when $u$ and $v$ are continuously\ndifferentiable, since a continuous function on $[a,b]$ is integrable\n([[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-derivative-of-a-power",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Powers are those of [[def-integer-power]], and $\\iota : \\mathbb{N} \\to\n\\mathbb{R}$ is the canonical natural of [[def-canonical-natural]], so that\n$\\iota(0) = 0$ and $\\iota(m+1) = \\iota(m) + 1$. Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\n1. The function $p_0 : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $p_0(x) = x^{0}$, is the\n   constant function $1$, and it is differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n   with $p_0'(c) = 0$ ([[def-derivative]]).\n2. For $n \\ge 1$ the function $p_n : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$,\n   $p_n(x) = x^{n}$, is differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and\n   $$p_n'(c) \\;=\\; \\iota(n)\\,c^{\\,n-1} .$$\n3. For $n \\ge 1$ put $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R} : x \\ne 0 \\,\\}$. The function\n   $m_n : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $m_n(x) = x^{-n}$, is differentiable at every\n   $c \\in A_0$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$m_n'(c) \\;=\\; -\\,\\iota(n)\\,c^{\\,-n-1} .$$\n4. Let $a : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with $a_j = 0$ for $j \\ge n$, and let\n   $P : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be the polynomial function\n   $P(x) = \\sum_{j<n} a_j x^{j}$ ([[def-finite-sum]]). Then $P$ is\n   differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and, defining $e : \\mathbb{N}\n   \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by $e_0 := 0$ and $e_j := \\iota(j)\\,a_j\\,c^{\\,j-1}$ for\n   $j \\ge 1$,\n   $$P'(c) \\;=\\; \\sum_{j<n} e_j .$$\n\n**Claim 2 is stated for $n \\ge 1$ and not for $n \\ge 0$, and that is not\ntimidity.** At $n = 0$ its right-hand side reads $\\iota(0)\\,c^{-1}$, and\n$c^{-1}$ is not defined at $c = 0$ ([[def-integer-power]]), so the formula is\nnot a statement about the whole line. Claim 1 is what covers $n = 0$, and it\nsays the derivative is $0$ there, which is what the informal reading\n\"$0 \\cdot c^{-1}$\" is reaching for. The same shift is why the term $e_0$ of\nclaim 4 is defined to be $0$ outright rather than by the formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $a<b$ and let $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ be\nbounded and Riemann integrable. Then $f$ is Lebesgue measurable on $[a,b]$ and\nis integrable there, and its Lebesgue integral equals its Riemann integral:\n$$\\int_{[a,b]} f\\,d\\lambda_1=\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx.$$\n\nThis is the point at which the completeness of Lebesgue measure is used\nessentially: the proof obtains a Borel function equal to $f$ almost everywhere,\nand measurability of $f$ itself is then a completeness statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-nonnegative-improper-riemann-integral-agrees-with-the-lebesgue-integral-on-a-half-line",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $a\\in\\mathbb R$ and let\n$f:[a,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$ be Riemann integrable on every compact interval\n$[a,R]$ with $R>a$. If the improper Riemann integral\n$$\\int_a^\\infty f(x)\\,dx$$\nconverges in the sense of [[def-improper-integral-at-infinity]], then $f$ is\nLebesgue integrable on $[a,\\infty)$ and\n$$\\int_{[a,\\infty)} f\\,d\\lambda_1=\\int_a^\\infty f(x)\\,dx.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The integrand $r\\mapsto r^3/(r^2+\\mu^2)$ is nonnegative and continuous on\n$[0,\\Lambda]$. Apply [F2], then divide by $(2\\pi)^4$, to obtain\n$$ I_\\Lambda=\\frac{1}{8\\pi^2}\\int_0^\\Lambda \\frac{r^3}{r^2+\\mu^2}\\,dr. $$\nPolynomial division gives\n$r^3/(r^2+\\mu^2)=r-\\mu^2r/(r^2+\\mu^2)$. By [F4], an antiderivative is\n$G(r)=r^2/2-(\\mu^2/2)\\log(r^2+\\mu^2)$ on this interval. Its derivative is\ncontinuous; its compact-interval integrands are bounded and Riemann integrable, so [F9] identifies their Lebesgue integrals with the calculus integrals. Thus [F3] evaluates the integral as\n$$ G(\\Lambda)-G(0) =\\frac12\\left(\\Lambda^2-\\mu^2\\log(1+\\Lambda^2/\\mu^2)\\right). $$\nMultiplying by $1/(8\\pi^2)$ gives the claimed expression.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "By [F6], $-e^{-x}$ has derivative $e^{-x}$; [F3] on $[0,R]$\ngives $\\int_0^R e^{-x}dx=1-e^{-R}$. The positive series [F5] gives\n$e^R\\ge1+R$, so $e^{-R}\\to0$ and $M_0=1$.\nFor each integer $n\\ge0$, [F5] with $y=x/2$ gives\n$x^ne^{-x}\\le2^n n!e^{-x/2}$. The majorant has finite integral $2^{n+1}n!$\nby [F3] and [F6]; the positive truncated integrals are increasing and bounded by this finite integral, hence have a finite improper limit. All the integrands are continuous on every compact interval; [F9] identifies these convergent nonnegative improper integrals with their half-line Lebesgue integrals. Thus $M_n=\\int_0^\\infty x^ne^{-x}dx$ is finite.\nThe same bound and $e^{x/2}\\ge1+x/2$ show $x^ne^{-x}\\to0$.\nFor $n\\ge1$ and $R>0$, take $u(x)=x^n$, $v(x)=-e^{-x}$ on $[0,R]$.\nThey are continuously differentiable with continuous, hence integrable,\nderivatives. Thus [F7] gives\n$$\\int_0^R x^ne^{-x}dx=-R^ne^{-R}+n\\int_0^R x^{n-1}e^{-x}dx.$$\nThe endpoint at zero vanishes since $n\\ge1$. Taking $R\\to\\infty$ is\njustified by the finite positive improper integrals and the vanishing\nboundary term just proved. It gives $M_n=nM_{n-1}$, and induction from\n$M_0=1$ gives $M_n=n!$. Finally\n$0\\le e^{-x}/(1+\\lambda x)\\le e^{-x}$ for $\\lambda\\ge0$, so $F(\\lambda)$\nis finite.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Substituting the formula from step 1.1, the subtracted quantity equals\n$$ \\frac{\\mu^2}{16\\pi^2} \\log\\left(\\frac{\\mu^2}{\\mu_R^2} \\frac{\\Lambda^2}{\\Lambda^2+\\mu^2}\\right). $$\nAs $\\Lambda\\to\\infty$, the second factor inside the logarithm tends to $1$;\nthe limiting logarithm is defined because $\\mu^2/\\mu_R^2>0$. Continuity of\n$\\log$ on $(0,\\infty)$ proves the stated finite limit.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "For every $t\\ge0$, finite multiplication of a geometric sum gives\n$$ \\frac1{1+t}=\\sum_{n=0}^N(-t)^n+ (-1)^{N+1}\\frac{t^{N+1}}{1+t}. $$\nPut $t=\\lambda x$, multiply by $e^{-x}$, and integrate. Step 1.2 evaluates\neach polynomial moment as $n!$, while the remainder is bounded by\n$\\lambda^{N+1}M_{N+1}=\\lambda^{N+1}(N+1)!$. This gives the exact finite\nidentity and its stated bound, including $\\lambda=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-3",
          "step": "2.3",
          "claim": "For $\\lambda>0$, the magnitudes of successive formal terms have ratio\n$\\lambda(n+1)$, which eventually exceeds $2$. Thus the terms do not tend to\nzero and the power series diverges at every positive $\\lambda$; the ratio\ntest for power-series radius therefore gives radius zero. Separately, the\nbare positive coefficient grows quadratically because\n$$ \\frac{I_\\Lambda}{\\Lambda^2} =\\frac1{16\\pi^2}\\left( 1-\\frac{\\mu^2}{\\Lambda^2}\\log(1+\\Lambda^2/\\mu^2)\\right) \\longrightarrow\\frac1{16\\pi^2}. $$\nFor the last limit, with $y=1+\\Lambda^2/\\mu^2$, [F4] gives\n$\\log y=\\int_1^y du/u\\le\\int_1^y du/\\sqrt u\\le2\\sqrt y$, so the logarithmic\nterm divided by $\\Lambda^2$ tends to zero.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Steps 1.1 and 2.1 give the exact cutoff integral and selected finite\nsubtraction limit; steps 1.2 and 2.2 prove the finite factorial remainder;\nstep 2.3 proves that the formal power series diverges for every $\\lambda>0$.\nThese conclusions concern only the two displayed mathematical integrals and\ndo not assert a full QED renormalization theorem or a continuum limit.",
          "inputs": [
