        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Finite UV subtraction and divergent factorial toy integral: Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and the unit convention of [[def-qft-example-data]]. Let $\\mu,\\Lambda,\\mu_R>0$ have units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. For $k\\in\\mathbb R^4$, $|k|$ is the Euclidean norm and $\\log$ is the natural logarithm. Define the cutoff integral $$ I_\\Lambda=\\int_{|k|\\le\\Lambda} \\frac{d^4k}{(2\\pi)^4(|k|^2+\\mu^2)}. $$ Each term in the subtracted expression has units $\\mathrm m^{-2}$. Then $$ I_\\Lambda= \\frac{\\Lambda^2-\\mu^2\\log(1+\\Lambda^2/\\mu^2)}{16\\pi^2}, $$ and the explicitly chosen subtraction has the finite limit $$ \\lim_{\\Lambda\\to\\infty} \\left(I_\\Lambda-\\frac{\\Lambda^2}{16\\pi^2} +\\frac{\\mu^2}{16\\pi^2}\\log\\frac{\\Lambda^2}{\\mu_R^2}\\right) =\\frac{\\mu^2}{16\\pi^2}\\log\\frac{\\mu^2}{\\mu_R^2}. $$ Separately,  The forward iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Finite UV subtraction and divergent factorial toy integral: Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and the unit convention of [[def-qft-example-data]]. Let $\\mu,\\Lambda,\\mu_R>0$ have units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. For $k\\in\\mathbb R^4$, $|k|$ is the Euclidean norm and $\\log$ is the natural logarithm. Define the cutoff integral $$ I_\\Lambda=\\int_{|k|\\le\\Lambda} \\frac{d^4k}{(2\\pi)^4(|k|^2+\\mu^2)}. $$ Each term in the subtracted expression has units $\\mathrm m^{-2}$. Then $$ I_\\Lambda= \\frac{\\Lambda^2-\\mu^2\\log(1+\\Lambda^2/\\mu^2)}{16\\pi^2}, $$ and the explicitly chosen subtraction has the finite limit $$ \\lim_{\\Lambda\\to\\infty} \\left(I_\\Lambda-\\frac{\\Lambda^2}{16\\pi^2} +\\frac{\\mu^2}{16\\pi^2}\\log\\frac{\\Lambda^2}{\\mu_R^2}\\right) =\\frac{\\mu^2}{16\\pi^2}\\log\\frac{\\mu^2}{\\mu_R^2}. $$ Separately,  The reverse iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        }
      ]
