    "thm-qft-example-soft-current-angular-coherent": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nUse the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. In the common natural-unit field convention, $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, a scalar field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and a Dirac field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation set $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$, with $A_a$ in tesla-metres and gauge function $\\chi$ in webers. With charge $q$ in coulombs define\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$$\\mathcal H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3),\\qquad \\langle u,v\\rangle_2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\overline{u(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,$$\nwith the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]; the real momentum dot product and norm are those of\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Explicitly extend the real dot product\ncomplex-bilinearly to $a\\cdot b=\\sum_{j=1}^3a_jb_j$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb C^3$.\nEach complex fiber instead has the positive Hermitian pairing\n$\\langle a,b\\rangle_2=\\overline a\\cdot b=\\sum_j\\overline a_jb_j$ and\nnorm $\\|a\\|^2=\\sum_j|a_j|^2$; the displayed integral uses this pairing.\nHilbert completeness follows from [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. For $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, write $n(\\mathbf k)=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and\n$$P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-n(\\mathbf k)n(\\mathbf k)^T.$$\nAssign any value at $\\mathbf k=0$, which is a Lebesgue-null set. Let $M_{P_T}$ be multiplication by this measurable matrix field and define the positive transverse photon space\n$$\\mathcal H_T=\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T} =\\{u\\in\\mathcal H:\\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for almost every }\\mathbf k\\ne0\\}.$$\nThe inner product on $\\mathcal H_T$ is the restriction of that on $\\mathcal H$. Each nonzero momentum fiber is the two-complex-dimensional plane $\\{a\\in\\mathbb C^3:\\mathbf k\\cdot a=0\\}$ perpendicular to the real vector $\\mathbf k$ in that Hermitian pairing; no global continuous polarization frame is part of the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms\n$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$\nfor all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of\n[[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$\nis the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in\n[[def-tempered-distribution]].\n\nFor every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear\nHilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$.\nFor each tensor product, use the same norm completion as\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged;\non elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings.\nLet $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with\n$H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of\nthe factor pairings. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and\n$P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the\npermutation action and the orthogonal projections of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define\n$H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and\n$H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with\n$H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric\n$n$-particle sectors.\n\nThe bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct\nsums\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and\n$\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product.\nTheir vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero\ncomponents.\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is\n$a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nbosonic annihilation is\n$a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where\n$C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$ and its tensor extension is the bounded map of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation\nby $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nfermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction\n$c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and\n$c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the\ncreation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the\nfinite-particle subspace; their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in\n[[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics\ntheorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ be its bosonic Fock space and finite-particle domain.\nFor $h\\in H$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the Segal field\n$$\\phi_0(h):=2^{-1/2}\\bigl(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h)\\bigr).$$\nFor $h\\ne0$, this symmetric operator is essentially self-adjoint; write\n$\\Phi(h)=\\overline{\\phi_0(h)}$. If $r=\\|h\\|$, $e=h/r$, $K=e^\\perp$,\n$G=\\mathcal F_s(K)$, choose a countable orthonormal basis $(u_j)$ of $G$\nwhose vectors are finite-particle, and use the one-mode splitting of\n[[lem-qft-fock-one-mode-splitting]] followed by the Hermite unitary\n$|n\\rangle\\mapsto g_n$ into $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C)$ with pairing\n$\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline f g$ to write a vector as\n$(f_j)_{j\\in J}\\in\\ell^2(J;L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C))$. Then the exact domain and\naction are\n$$D(\\Phi(h))=\\left\\{(f_j):\\sum_{j\\in J}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+r^2x^2)|f_j(x)|^2\\,dx<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad (\\Phi(h)f)_j(x)=rx f_j(x).$$\nFor $h=0$, $\\phi_0(0)$ closes to the zero operator on all of $\\mathcal F_s(H)$.\n\nDefine $\\epsilon(z)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}z^{\\otimes n}/\\sqrt{n!}$ for $z\\in H$.\nThere are unitaries $W(h)$ determined on these total exponential vectors by\n$$W(h)\\epsilon(z)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|h\\|^2}{4} +\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}\\langle h,z\\rangle_2\\right) \\epsilon\\!\\left(z+\\frac{i h}{\\sqrt2}\\right).$$\nThey obey\n$$W(h)W(k)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{i}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2\\right)W(h+k),\\qquad \\langle\\Omega,W(h)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|h\\|^2/4},$$\nand $h\\mapsto W(h)$ is strongly continuous. For every $h$, the one-parameter\ngroup $t\\mapsto W(th)$ has norm derivative at zero exactly on\n$D(\\Phi(h))$, with derivative $i\\Phi(h)$ (for $h=0$ this is the zero\ngenerator). Thus the displayed bounded Weyl operators have precisely the\nclosed Segal fields as their generators; no point-field or measurement claim\nis included.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space and let\n$\\boldsymbol\\omega=(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_d):X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a\nfinite-valued measurable map, where $d\\ge1$. Put\n$H=L^2(X,\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ with the second-variable-linear pairing of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and form the bosonic Fock space\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)$. For $n\\ge1$, identify $H_s^{(n)}$ with the symmetric\nsubspace of $L^2(X^n,\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}};\\mathbb C)$ by iterating\n[[lem-qft-l2-product-tensor-identification]]. Set $X^0=\\{\\star\\}$,\n$s_0(\\star)=0$, and for $n\\ge1$ set\n$$s_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\sum_{r=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\omega(x_r)\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$\nFor $1\\le j\\le d$, define $H_j$ by multiplication by the $j$th coordinate\n$s_{n,j}$ on sector $n$, with exact domain\n$$D(H_j)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n): \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{X^n}|s_{n,j}|^2|\\Psi_n|^2\\,d\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}}<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nEach $H_j$ is self-adjoint, and $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\cap D(H_j)$ is a\ngraph core. The joint projection-valued measure\n[[def-projection-valued-measure]] on $\\mathbb R^d$ is\n$$(E(B)\\Psi)_n=\\mathbf1_{s_n^{-1}(B)}\\Psi_n\\quad(n\\ge1),\\qquad (E(B)\\Psi)_0=\\mathbf1_B(0)\\Psi_0.$$\nFor $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^d$, let $H_\\theta$ be multiplication on sector $n$\nby $\\theta\\cdot s_n$, with its maximal square-integrability domain. Then\n$$U_\\theta(t)\\Psi:=\\bigl(e^{it\\theta\\cdot s_n}\\Psi_n\\bigr)_{n\\ge0}$$\nis a strongly continuous unitary group on all of Fock space, and its norm\nderivative at zero exists exactly for $\\Psi\\in D(H_\\theta)$, where it is\n$iH_\\theta\\Psi$. The zero-particle sector is the vacuum eigenvector at joint\nvalue $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention of [[def-bounded-c-one-domain-boundary-charts-and-outward-normal]]. Let S be a compact embedded $C^1$ hypersurface in $\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge2$. Choose finitely many regular injective $C^1$ parametrizations $X_j:V_j\\to S\\cap O_j$ with $C^1$ coordinate transitions, and an ambient partition $\\chi_j$ from [[lem-finite-ambient-partitions-for-euclidean-boundary-integration]], with supports compactly contained in the corresponding chart neighborhoods. Put $J_{X_j}=\\sqrt{\\det(DX_j^TDX_j)}$, using [[def-gram-matrix-and-gram-determinant]].\n\nFor a nonnegative Borel f on S define $\\int_S f\\,dS=\\sum_j\\int_{V_j}(\\chi_j f)(X_j(y))J_{X_j}(y)\\,dy$, where the integrals on the right are those of [[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]] and $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Set $S(A)=\\int_S\\mathbf1_A\\,dS$ for Borel A. For signed f with $\\int_S|f|\\,dS<\\infty$ use the difference of the positive and negative integrals. The same chart formula restricts to a compact Borel face contained in a regular patch. The empty surface has zero integral. Independence and finiteness are established by the following chart-independence lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and $n\\ge2$. The chart surface measure on $S^{n-1}$ equals the polar measure $\\sigma$. Orthogonal transformations preserve it. The map $\\omega\\mapsto a+R\\omega$, $R>0$, multiplies surface measure by $R^{n-1}$, and $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n \\ge 1$,\nlet $S^{n-1} \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be the unit sphere, and let $\\sigma$ be the\nset function of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]. Then $\\sigma$\nis a finite Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$, and for every Borel measurable\n$f : \\mathbb R^n \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^n} f(x)\\,d\\lambda_n(x) = \\int_0^\\infty \\int_{S^{n-1}} f(r\\omega) r^{n-1}\\,d\\sigma(\\omega)\\,dr.$$\nMoreover, $\\sigma$ is the unique Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$ with this\nproperty.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $m\\ge1$, let U,V be open subsets of $\\mathbb R^m$, and let $T:U\\to V$ be a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Borel $h:V\\to[0,\\infty]$, $\\int_V h(y)\\,dy=\\int_U h(T(x))|\\det DT(x)|\\,dx$, with equality in $[0,\\infty]$ and $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. This statement concerns Borel h; no completed-measurable substitution is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-rational-box-step-functions-form-a-countable-dense-subset-of-l-p-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice.\n\nLet $1 \\le p < \\infty$. The finite linear combinations of indicator functions of\nhalf-open boxes with rational endpoints and rational coefficients form a\ncountable dense subset of $L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Then:\n\n1. **Degenerate boxes.** If $a_i \\le b_i$ are reals with $a_{i_0} = b_{i_0}$ for\n   some $i_0 < n$, then every set $R$ between the open box\n   $\\{x : a_i < x_i < b_i\\ (i<n)\\}$ and the closed rectangle $[a,b]$ is Lebesgue\n   measurable with $\\lambda_n(R) = 0$.\n2. **Coordinate hyperplanes.** For $i_0 < n$ and a real $c$, the set\n   $$H_{i_0,c} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : x_{i_0} = c \\,\\}$$\n   is Lebesgue measurable with $\\lambda_n(H_{i_0,c}) = 0$.\n\nAt $n = 1$ the hyperplane $H_{0,c}$ is the singleton $\\{c\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-qft-qed-coherent-ir-obstruction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the transverse one-particle space $\\mathcal H_T$ and multiplier $\\omega(\\mathbf k)$ of [[def-qft-qed-photon-energy-multiplier]], and form its symmetric Fock space $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$. Let $H_{\\rm ph}:=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma(\\omega)$, with $\\hbar,c>0$ and $d\\Gamma(\\omega)$ acting on the $n$-particle sector as multiplication by $\\sum_{j=1}^n|\\mathbf k_j|$, as in [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]. For a coherent vector use\n$$ \\epsilon(f):=\\bigoplus_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{f^{\\otimes n}}{\\sqrt{n!}},\\qquad C(f):=e^{-\\|f\\|^2/2}\\epsilon(f), $$\nusing the tensor and direct-sum conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]].\n\nLet $\\kappa>0$. Let $b:S^2\\to\\mathbb C^3$ be measurable with $b\\in L^2(S^2,d\\Omega;\\mathbb C^3)$, $\\mathbf n\\cdot b(\\mathbf n)=0$ for a.e. $\\mathbf n$, and\n$$ C_b:=\\int_{S^2}|b(\\mathbf n)|^2\\,d\\Omega>0. $$\nTake $\\mathbf k,\\kappa,\\epsilon$ in inverse metres and $b$ dimensionless; then $f_\\epsilon$ has momentum-wavefunction units $\\mathrm m^{3/2}$, $C_b$ is dimensionless, and $\\hbar c(\\kappa-\\epsilon)$ has energy units.\nFor $0<\\epsilon<\\kappa$, define $f_\\epsilon(0)=0$ and\n$$ f_\\epsilon(\\mathbf k):=\\mathbf1_{\\{\\epsilon<|\\mathbf k|<\\kappa\\}}|\\mathbf k|^{-3/2}b\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf k}{|\\mathbf k|}\\right). $$\nThen\n$$ \\|f_\\epsilon\\|^2=C_b\\log\\frac{\\kappa}{\\epsilon},\\qquad \\langle C(f_\\epsilon),H_{\\rm ph}C(f_\\epsilon)\\rangle =\\hbar c\\,C_b(\\kappa-\\epsilon). $$\nEach $C(f_\\epsilon)$ is a unit vector in $D(H_{\\rm ph})$. As $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$, $C(f_\\epsilon)\\rightharpoonup0$ weakly, but the family has no strong Fock-space limit. The statement is an exact free transverse-mode construction; it asserts no interacting charged sector, continuum QED limit, or empirical observation.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-product-of-countable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $A$ and $B$ are at most countable ([[def-countable]]) then so is\n$A \\times B$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A$ be a nonempty set. Then $A$ is at most countable ([[def-countable]]) if\nand only if there is a surjection $s : \\mathbb{N} \\to A$\n([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For every leg and $n\\in S^2$,\n$1-v_i\\cdot n\\ge1-|v_i|>0$. Since the leg set is finite, each summand of\n$K(n)$ is continuous and bounded on $S^2$. Moreover\n$$ h(K(n),(1,n))= \\sum_i\\eta_iQ_i\\frac{1-v_i\\cdot n}{1-v_i\\cdot n} =\\sum_i\\eta_iQ_i=0. $$\nThus $K^0(n)=n\\cdot\\mathbf K(n)$ and\n$h(K,K)=(K^0)^2-|\\mathbf K|^2=-|P_T(n)\\mathbf K(n)|^2$.\nConsequently the angular integrand is nonnegative, bounded and measurable;\nthe coefficient $C$ is finite and nonnegative.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F6] and [F7], the $d\\Omega$ used in the statement is the finite\nEuclidean surface measure, and [F8] gives\n$d^3q/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega)=\n\\omega\\,d\\omega\\,d\\Omega/(2(2\\pi)^3)$. Multiplying by\n$|h_\\lambda(\\mathbf q)|^2=g^2|K_\\perp(n)|^2/\\omega^2$ and integrating over\n$\\lambda<\\omega<\\Lambda$ gives\n$$ \\|h_\\lambda\\|^2= \\frac{g^2}{2(2\\pi)^3}\\int_{S^2}|K_\\perp|^2d\\Omega \\int_\\lambda^\\Lambda\\frac{d\\omega}{\\omega} =C\\log(\\Lambda/\\lambda). $$\nThe substitution $\\omega=\\lambda u$ and [F15] give the displayed logarithm.\nThe cutoffs are strictly positive and finite, so the norm is finite. The\nendpoint set $\\{\\lambda,\\Lambda\\}$ is null by [F11], so the strict cutoff\nconvention gives the same integral as including either endpoint.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F11",
            "F15",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "By [F10] at $p=2$, the real-valued scalar space $L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$ has a countable dense set $D$. It is nonempty because this scalar $L^2$ space is nonempty and $D$ is dense. By [F12], $D\\times D$ is at most countable; [F14] supplies a surjection from $\\mathbb N$ onto this nonempty product. Composing with $(d,e)\\mapsto d+ie$ and applying [F14] again shows that the nonempty set $D_{\\mathbb C}:=\\{d+ie:d,e\\in D\\}$ is at most countable. Every complex scalar $u\\in L^2$ has real and imaginary parts $u_R,u_I\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$, and\n$$\\|u-(d+ie)\\|_2^2=\\|u_R-d\\|_2^2+\\|u_I-e\\|_2^2.$$\nSince $D$ is dense, for each $\\varepsilon>0$ choose $d,e\\in D$ with both squared errors less than $\\varepsilon^2/2$; hence $D_{\\mathbb C}$ is dense in complex scalar $L^2$. Applying [F12] twice, the nonempty product $D_{\\mathbb C}^3$ is at most countable. It is dense in $L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^3)$: for $z=(z_1,z_2,z_3)$ and $\\varepsilon>0$, approximate each component by some $w_j\\in D_{\\mathbb C}$ with squared error less than $\\varepsilon^2/3$, so the sum of squared errors is less than $\\varepsilon^2$. For $n\\in S^2$ and $z\\in\\mathbb C^3$, put $a=\\sum_jn_jz_j$. Using [F13] and realness of $n_j$, direct expansion gives\n$$\\|P_T(n)z\\|^2=\\sum_j|z_j-n_ja|^2=\\sum_j|z_j|^2-|a|^2\\le\\|z\\|^2,$$\nand $(I-nn^T)^2=I-nn^T$ because $\\sum_jn_j^2=1$. Integrating the pointwise norm bound gives $\\|M_{P_T}u\\|_2\\le\\|u\\|_2$, and the matrix identity gives $M_{P_T}^2=M_{P_T}$; hence this multiplier is a bounded idempotent on the ambient $L^2$ space. Given $z\\in\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T}$ and $\\varepsilon>0$, choose $w\\in D_{\\mathbb C}^3$ with $\\|w-z\\|_2<\\varepsilon$. Contractivity and $M_{P_T}z=z$ give $\\|M_{P_T}w-z\\|_2\\le\\|w-z\\|_2<\\varepsilon$. The nonempty image $M_{P_T}(D_{\\mathbb C}^3)$ is at most countable: [F14] gives a surjection from $\\mathbb N$ onto $D_{\\mathbb C}^3$, whose composition with $M_{P_T}$ surjects onto the image, so [F14] applies. This image is dense in the current space [F2], proving that space separable as required by [F4]. Define\n$$ (Uh)(k)=\\frac{\\hbar c}{\\sqrt{(2\\pi)^3\\,2|k|}}h(\\hbar ck)\\quad(k\\ne0). $$\nSet $Uh(0)=0$, which does not affect its $L^2$ class. The linear change $\\mathbf q=\\hbar ck$ has Jacobian $(\\hbar c)^3$ by [F9], and $|\\mathbf q|=\\hbar c|k|$; substitution proves $\\|Uh\\|_{L^2(d^3k)}=\\|h\\|$. Its inverse is\n$h(\\mathbf q)=\\sqrt{(2\\pi)^3\\,2|\\mathbf q|}\\,(\\hbar c)^{-3/2}(Uh)(\\mathbf q/(\\hbar c))$, so $U$ is onto the ambient length-coordinate $L^2$ space. Both formulas\nmultiply by a nonzero scalar and scale the same spatial momentum by\n$\\hbar c>0$, so $\\mathbf q\\cdot h(\\mathbf q)=0$ a.e. is equivalent to\n$k\\cdot(Uh)(k)=0$ a.e. Thus their restrictions give a unitary\n$U:\\mathcal H_{E,T}\\to\\mathcal H_T$, with the displayed inverse restricted\nto $\\mathcal H_T$. Since $K_\\perp(n)$ is transverse, $Uh_\\lambda$ belongs to the range in [F2]. Substitution of the formula for $h_\\lambda$ gives exactly\n$$ (Uh_\\lambda)(k)=\\mathbf1_{\\{\\lambda/(\\hbar c)<|k|<\\Lambda/(\\hbar c)\\}} |k|^{-3/2}\\frac{gK_\\perp(k/|k|)}{\\sqrt{2(2\\pi)^3}}. $$\nIts angular coefficient has squared integral $C$ by the definition of $C$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F12",
            "F13",
            "F14",
