          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Put $N=N_\\lambda$. The series for $\\epsilon(f_\\lambda)$ has squared\nsector norms $N^m/m!$, so its total squared norm is $e^N$. Setting\n$h=-i\\sqrt2 f_\\lambda$ and $z=0$ in [F4] yields\n$W(h)\\Omega=e^{-N/2}\\epsilon(f_\\lambda)$, hence $\\Psi_\\lambda$ is\nnormalized and its sector squared norms are\n$s_m=e^{-N}N^m/m!$. The exponential series gives\n$\\sum_ms_m=1$ and, by shifting the index,\n$\\sum_m m s_m=N$. These are norm identities only; no measurement\ninterpretation is used.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "On the support, the one-photon energy $\\omega_\\gamma(k)=\\hbar c|k|$\nsatisfies $0<\\omega_\\gamma<\\Lambda$. By [F5], the energy on sector $m$ is\nthe sum of its $m$ one-photon energies, bounded above by $m\\Lambda$.\nThe same exponential series gives\n$\\sum_m m^2s_m=N^2+N<\\infty$, so\n$\\sum_m\\| (H_\\gamma\\Psi_\\lambda)_m\\|^2\n\\le\\Lambda^2(N^2+N)$ and the vector lies in the operator domain.\nFor each $m\\ge1$, the sector quadratic form of the unnormalized tensor\n$f_\\lambda^{\\otimes m}/\\sqrt{m!}$ is\n$m\\langle f_\\lambda,\\omega_\\gamma f_\\lambda\\rangle\nN^{m-1}/m!$. Summing with the factor $e^{-N}$ gives\n$\\langle\\Psi_\\lambda,H_\\gamma\\Psi_\\lambda\\rangle\n=\\langle f_\\lambda,\\omega_\\gamma f_\\lambda\\rangle$. In energy coordinates\nthe latter integral is\n$$ \\frac{g^2}{2(2\\pi)^3}\\int_{S^2}|K_\\perp|^2d\\Omega \\int_\\lambda^\\Lambda d\\omega=C(\\Lambda-\\lambda). $$\nThis is a free-model Hilbert-space expectation in joules, not an empirical\nmeasurement.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "If $C=0$, the nonnegative integral defining $C$ gives $K_\\perp=0$ almost\neverywhere, so $h_\\lambda=f_\\lambda=0$ as Hilbert-space vectors and\n$\\Psi_\\lambda=\\Omega$. If $C>0$, the function\n$b=gK_\\perp/\\sqrt{2(2\\pi)^3}$ is a nonzero transverse member of\n$L^2(S^2;\\mathbb C^3)$, with squared norm integral $C$. Its cutoffs\n$\\epsilon=\\lambda/(\\hbar c)$ and $\\kappa=\\Lambda/(\\hbar c)$ satisfy\n$0<\\epsilon<\\kappa$, and step 3.1 identifies its exact wavefunction with\n[L1]'s current family. Conditional on [L1], its weak-zero and no-strong-limit\nclaims apply, and its energy formula agrees with step 5.1 because\n$\\hbar cC(\\kappa-\\epsilon)=C(\\Lambda-\\lambda)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-7-1",
          "step": "7.1",
          "claim": "To prove the projective-ray claim separately, put\n$\\epsilon_j=\\kappa2^{-j}$ and $f_j=f_{\\epsilon_j}$. The increment\n$f_{j+1}-f_j$ is supported on the disjoint shell\n$\\epsilon_{j+1}<|k|<\\epsilon_j$, so its squared norm is\n$C\\log2>0$, using [F15] on this radial shell. Also\n$\\langle f_j,f_{j+1}\\rangle=\\|f_j\\|^2$, because the increment is orthogonal\nto $f_j$. By [F4] and the normalization in step 4.1,\n$|\\langle\\Psi_j,\\Psi_{j+1}\\rangle|=e^{-C\\log2/2}<1$. For every phase $\\theta$,\n$$ \\|\\Psi_{j+1}-e^{i\\theta}\\Psi_j\\|^2 =2-2\\operatorname{Re}(e^{-i\\theta}\\langle\\Psi_j,\\Psi_{j+1}\\rangle) \\ge2(1-e^{-C\\log2/2})>0. $$\nThus adjacent rays remain a fixed positive projective distance apart, so\nthe ray sequence is not Cauchy and has no projective limit: phases making the unit vectors converge in norm to a fixed unit representative would force adjacent phase-adjusted differences to tend to zero, contradicting this uniform lower bound. This fills the\ninterface difference in [L1] without asserting that its current statement\ncontains a projective-ray result.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "F15",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
